{"id":"id-1792322870228","object":"chat.completion","created":1792322870,"model":"@cf/openai/gpt-oss-120b","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"V1 Verdikt: A — Begründung: Ein zentral erzeugter Live‑Index liefert einen eindeutigen, maschinenlesbaren Zustand, den jede Sitzung zuerst einliest; Prosa‑Docs bleiben erhalten, fungieren aber nur noch als Zeiger. — Riss: Derzeit fehlt ein automatisierter Index‑Generator, sodass Aufwand und Implementierungs‑Risiko ungemessen bleiben.  \n\nV2 Verdikt: A — Begründung: Die Dokumentation 44‑Schwarm definiert eindeutig den kanonischen Schwarm; die 4‑Sitz‑Läufe wurden als Fehler gekennzeichnet und dürfen nicht als Schwarm gelten. — Riss: In Sonderfällen (z. B. Test‑Runs) wird gelegentlich bewusst ein kleinerer Schwarm eingesetzt, was nicht im Kanon abgebildet ist.  \n\nV3 Verdikt: A — Begründung: Ein verpflichtender Start‑of‑Session‑Check (z. B. `git log -5` + aktuelle state‑Dateien) stellt sicher, dass nur aktuelle Quellen zitiert werden. — Riss: Der zusätzliche Check kann die Sitzungs‑Startzeit erhöhen und wird bei sehr hohen Durchsatz‑Anforderungen möglicherweise als zu kostenintensiv angesehen.","refusal":null,"annotations":null,"audio":null,"function_call":null,"reasoning":"We need to answer V1, V2, V3 each with format:\n\n`V<x> Verdikt: <A|B|C|D> — Begründung: <zwei Sätze> — Riss: <stärkster Gegenbeleg oder ungemessene Größe>`\n\nWe must not invent facts, be concise, German, two sentences for Begründung. Provide strongest counter-evidence or unmeasured size for Riss.\n\nWe need to decide which option is best for each question.\n\nV1: Where should live state be? Options: A (generated live index that each session reads first; prose docs become pointers). B (prose docs stay but with mandatory header commit). C (no index; each session reads git log + latest run outputs). D (status quo + discipline). Considering causes: need live index to avoid drift. Best is A. Provide reasoning: central authoritative source, reduces duplication. Riss: existing infrastructure may not support auto-generation, unknown overhead.\n\nV2: What is canonical swarm? Options: A (always 44 free44 list; 4 not swarm). B (4 measured seats per rat-anweisung). C (adaptive based on health). D (no free swarm; only UI+Paid). Considering cause: need canonical definition; documentation says 44 is correct, 4 is error. So A is best.\n\nRiss: occasional experiments with smaller swarms for testing; not captured.\n\nV3: How prevent outdated citations? Options: A (start-of-session check). B (gate preventing citing older docs). C (generated index makes old docs visible). D (discipline). Best is A or B? A is proactive check; B is gate enforcement. Both similar. Which is stronger? A includes git log -5 + latest state, seems concrete. So choose A.\n\nRiss: human error may still cite older docs despite check; also performance cost.\n\nNow produce answers.","reasoning_content":"We need to answer V1, V2, V3 each with format:\n\n`V<x> Verdikt: <A|B|C|D> — Begründung: <zwei Sätze> — Riss: <stärkster Gegenbeleg oder ungemessene Größe>`\n\nWe must not invent facts, be concise, German, two sentences for Begründung. Provide strongest counter-evidence or unmeasured size for Riss.\n\nWe need to decide which option is best for each question.\n\nV1: Where should live state be? Options: A (generated live index that each session reads first; prose docs become pointers). B (prose docs stay but with mandatory header commit). C (no index; each session reads git log + latest run outputs). D (status quo + discipline). Considering causes: need live index to avoid drift. Best is A. Provide reasoning: central authoritative source, reduces duplication. Riss: existing infrastructure may not support auto-generation, unknown overhead.\n\nV2: What is canonical swarm? Options: A (always 44 free44 list; 4 not swarm). B (4 measured seats per rat-anweisung). C (adaptive based on health). D (no free swarm; only UI+Paid). Considering cause: need canonical definition; documentation says 44 is correct, 4 is error. So A is best.\n\nRiss: occasional experiments with smaller swarms for testing; not captured.\n\nV3: How prevent outdated citations? Options: A (start-of-session check). B (gate preventing citing older docs). C (generated index makes old docs visible). D (discipline). Best is A or B? A is proactive check; B is gate enforcement. Both similar. Which is stronger? A includes git log -5 + latest state, seems concrete. So choose A.\n\nRiss: human error may still cite older docs despite check; also performance cost.\n\nNow produce answers."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop","stop_reason":null,"token_ids":null,"routed_experts":null}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":887,"completion_tokens":666,"total_tokens":1553,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0},"neurons":73.63163757324219}}